      <!-- Question 18: CTA Reveal Interaction End -->
      <para id="para-00025">In the function \(f(bx)\), the \(b\) is acting as the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">horizontal stretch</term> or <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00003">horizontal compression</term> of the parent function. It dilates the graph.</para>
      <list id="list-00007">
        <item>When \(b\) is negative, there is also a <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00004">horizontal reflection</term> of the graph over the \(y\)-axis.</item>
        <item>Multiplying the function's input by \(b\) horizontally compresses the graph of \(f\) by a factor of \(b\) units if \(b > 1\).</item>
        <item>The \(b\) horizontally expands the graph of \(f\) by a factor of \(b\) units if \(0 \lt b \lt 1\).</item>
      </list>
      
        <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_12_06.png"/>
        </media>
      
      <note id="note-00002">
        <title>Horizontal Dilations of a Function</title>
        <para id="para-00026">A horizontal stretch or compression “transforms” the parent function into another function by horizontally dilating the graph by “b” units.</para>
        <para id="para-00027"> Horizontal Dilation → \( f(bx) \)</para>
      </note>
      <para id="para-00028">If the \(b\) value is negative, the graph is reflected over the \(y\)-axis. If the \(b\) value is greater than 1, the graph is horizontally compressed. If the \(b\) value is between 0 and 1, the graph is horizontally stretched.</para>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00003"><title>Self Check</title>

      <para id="para-00029">
        Which function represents \(g(x)\), a function transformed from \(f(x)\) by a horizontal compression?
     </para>
      </note>
      <section id="sect-00002">
        <title>Additional Resources</title>
        
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Horizontal Transformations of Linear Functions</title>
      <para id="para-00030">Horizontal transformations are the result of altering the input values before applying the function rule. Vertical transformations result from altering the output values after the function rule has been applied.</para>
      <title>Example 1</title>
      <para id="para-00031">Describe how \( f(x) \) is transformed into \( f(x - 3) \).</para>
      <para id="para-00032">
       <emphasis>Solution:</emphasis>
    </para>
      <para id="para-00033">First, graph the given function, \( f(x) \), the parent linear function.</para>
      
        <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_14_01.png"/>
        </media>
      
      <para id="para-00034">Now, let’s examine an input value of \(x=2\) using \(f(x)\).</para>
      <list id="list-00008">
        <item>For the parent function \(f(x)\), all points on the line are represented by \((x, f(x))\).</item>
        <item>So, when \(x = 2\), then \(f(x)\) becomes \(f(2)\).</item>
        <item>That means this point is located at \((2, f(2))\) or \((2, 2)\).</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00035">In order to get the same output value for \(f(x-3)\) as we had for \(f(x)\), we need to figure out how to change the input value. 
</para>
      <list id="list-00009">
        <item>Because our transformed equation is subtracting three from what we input, we have to increase the prior input value by 3 units to yield the same output as \(f(x)\).</item>
        <item>The input value that is 3 units larger than \(x = 2\) is 5. In the transformed equation, we must use \(x=5\).</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00036">So, in the original equation where \(x = 2\) resulted in \(f(2) = 2\), in the transformed equation, we have to use \(x = 5\) in order for \(f(x-3) = f(5-3) = f(2)\).</para>
      
        <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_14_02.png"/>
        </media>
      
      <para id="para-00037">On the graph, \(x = 5\) is located 3 units to the right of \(x = 2\). So, the transformed equation of \(f(x-3)\) is 3 units to the right of \(f(x)\).</para>
      <para id="para-00038">Horizontal transformations are represented by the rule \(f(x - c)\) where \(c\) indicates the number of units to the right or left that the graph is shifted.</para>
      <title>Example 2</title>
      <para id="para-00039">Graph \( f(x + 2) \) when \( f(x) = \frac{1}{2}x - 3 \). Then, determine the equation of the transformed function.</para>
      <para id="para-00040">
       <emphasis>Solution:</emphasis>
    </para>
      <para id="para-00041"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Graph the given function, \( f(x) = \frac{1}{2}x - 3 \).</para>
      <para id="para-00042">
    The <emphasis effect="italics">y</emphasis>-intercept is (0, -3) and the slope is \(\frac{1}{2}\).
    
    For horizontal shifts, it is helpful to identify the <emphasis effect="italics">x</emphasis>-intercept. On our graph, the <emphasis effect="italics">x</emphasis>-intercept or zero is located at (6,0). 
</para>
      <para id="para-00043">
          <media alt="RAISE 4.11.5 AR Ex0 graph">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_14_03.png"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00044"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Apply the designated transformation. Remember that the rule for a horizontal shift is \( f(x - c) \). If we rewrite \( f(x + 2) \) in an equivalent form, we can make it is easier to see \( x - c \).</para>
      <para id="para-00045">\( f(x + 2) = f(x - (-2)) \) That means \( c = -2 \).</para>
      <para id="para-00046">So, \( f(x + 2) \) represents a horizontal shift of two units to the left. On our graph, we move the <emphasis effect="italics">x</emphasis>-intercept two units left from (6, 0) to (4, 0).</para>
      <para id="para-00047">
          <media alt="Transformed Function Graph">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_14_04.png"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00048">The orange dashed line is the graph of the original function and the blue solid line is the transformed function.</para>
      <para id="para-00049"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Determine the equation of the transformed function.</para>
      <para id="para-00050">The <emphasis effect="italics">y</emphasis>-intercept of the transformed line is (0, -2) and the slope of the line is \(\frac{2}{4}\) or \(\frac{1}{2}\).</para>
      <para id="para-00051">That means the equation \( f(x + 2) = \frac{1}{2}x - 2 \)</para>
      <para id="para-00052">Want to check your work? We can check our equation algebraically by substituting \( x + 2 \) into \( f(x) \).</para>
      <para id="para-00053">\( f(x) = \frac{1}{2}x - 3 \)</para>
      <para id="para-00054">\( f(x+2) = \frac{1}{2}(x + 2) - 3 = \frac{1}{2}x + 1 - 3 = \frac{1}{2}x - 2 \) ✔</para>
      <title>Example 3</title>
      <para id="para-00055">Graph \( f(4x) \) when \( f(x) = \frac{1}{2}x - 3 \). Then, determine the equation of the transformed function.</para>
      <para id="para-00056">
       <emphasis>Solution:</emphasis>
    </para>
      <para id="para-00057"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Graph the given function, \( f(x) = \frac{1}{2}x - 3 \).</para>
      <para id="para-00058">The <emphasis effect="italics">y</emphasis>-intercept is (0, -3) and the slope is \(\frac{1}{2}\).</para>
      <para id="para-00059">For horizontal shifts, it is helpful to identify the <emphasis effect="italics">x</emphasis>-intercept. On our graph, the <emphasis effect="italics">x</emphasis>-intercept or zero is located at (6,0).</para>
      <para id="para-00060">
          <media alt="Graph of f(4x)">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_14_05.png"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00061"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Apply the designated transformation. Remember that the rule for a horizontal shift is \( f(bx) \). And, when the value of \( b \) is greater than 1, the function experiences a horizontal compression.</para>
      <para id="para-00062">As a result of the horizontal compression, the linear function should look steeper because it is being compressed by a scale factor of 4.</para>
      <para id="para-00063">On our graph, this means the <emphasis effect="italics">x</emphasis>-intercept is compressed from (6, 0) to (1.5, 0).</para>
      <para id="para-00064">
          <media alt="Graph of f(4x)">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_14_06.png"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00065">The orange dashed line is the graph of the original function and the blue solid line is the transformed function.</para>
      <para id="para-00066"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Determine the equation of the transformed function.</para>
      <para id="para-00067">The <emphasis effect="italics">y</emphasis>-intercept of the transformed line is (0, -3) and the slope of the line is 3/1.5 or 2.</para>
      <para id="para-00068">That means the equation \( f(4x) = 2x - 3 \).</para>
      <para id="para-00069">Want to check your work? We can check our equation algebraically by substituting 4x into \( f(x) \).</para>
      <para id="para-00070">\( f(x) = \frac{1}{2}x - 3 \)</para>
      <para id="para-00071">\( f(4x) = \frac{1}{2}(4x) - 3 = 2x - 3 \) ✔</para>
      <note class="try" id="note-00004">
  <title>Try It: Horizontal Transformations of Linear Functions</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00011">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            <para id="para-00072">Name the transformation to go from the parent function, \( f(x) = x \), to \( f(x - 1) \).</para>
            <para id="para-00073">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00074">Here is how to determine the transformation:</para>
            <para id="para-00075">Because \( f(x - c) \) defines a horizontal shift, the transformation \( f(x - 1) \) moves the parent function 1 unit to the right.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
      </section>
      
  </content>
